window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"p":"architecture.html","a":"why-regulate","t":"Why regulate at all","s":"Load current can jump from microamps to amps in nanoseconds; the input moves as the battery discharges, a charger is plugged in or an engine cranks. A regulator is a negative-feedback system: it measures the output, compares it with a refer","g":"arch","lv":1},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"linear-vs-switching","t":"Linear vs switching regulation","s":"A linear regulator runs its pass element in the saturation / linear region, acting as a variable resistor controlled by feedback. The excess voltage (VIN − VOUT) times the current all becomes heat. It is simple, quiet and fast, and needs no","g":"arch","lv":1},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"ldo-block","t":"The LDO block diagram","s":"Reference: usually a bandgap with a tempco below 50 ppm/°C; it sets the output accuracy. Error amplifier: its gain sets regulation, its bandwidth sets transient response, and its output swing decides whether the pass device can be fully tur","g":"arch","lv":1},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"pass-device","t":"Pass device: PMOS, NMOS or PNP","s":"Pass deviceMinimum dropoutGate driveLoop behaviourTypical use PMOS (common source)I·RDS(on), down to 50–200 mVPull the gate low; no voltage above VIN neededHigh output impedance; dominant pole at the output; needs COUT / ESR for stabilityGe","g":"arch","lv":1},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"dropout","t":"Dropout, efficiency and power dissipation","s":"Dropout voltage (VDO) is the minimum VIN − VOUT at which the output still holds its setpoint, usually defined where the output has fallen 1–2% or 100 mV. In dropout, a PMOS LDO's pass device is fully on and behaves as a resistor: VDO ≈ IOUT","g":"arch","lv":1},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"ldo-specs","t":"Key LDO specifications","s":"","g":"arch","lv":1},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"ldo-specs","t":"Output accuracy","s":"The sum of reference, divider, EA offset, temperature, line and load regulation; typically ±1% (25 °C) or ±2% (full temperature).","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"ldo-specs","t":"Quiescent current IQ","s":"Current the LDO draws itself at no load. Wearables and IoT want < 1 µA; high-PSRR, high-bandwidth LDOs may need tens to hundreds of µA.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"ldo-specs","t":"Ground current IGND","s":"Current to ground under load; close to IQ for PMOS LDOs, rising with load for PNP LDOs.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"ldo-specs","t":"Shutdown current ISD","s":"Leakage with EN low; sets standby battery life, typically < 100 nA.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"ldo-specs","t":"PSRR","s":"How much input ripple is rejected, in dB, falling with frequency. See Design · PSRR.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"ldo-specs","t":"Output noise","s":"Integrated noise from 10 Hz to 100 kHz, in µVRMS. Low-noise LDOs reach < 5 µVRMS.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"ldo-specs","t":"Stable capacitor range","s":"The minimum COUT and ESR range in the datasheet. Outside it the LDO may oscillate — the most common application problem.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"ldo-specs","t":"Current limit / short-circuit current","s":"Maximum current into a short; may be constant-current or foldback.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"buck","t":"The buck converter","s":"D = VOUT/VIN (ideal) ΔIL = VOUT(1 − D) / (L·fSW) ΔVOUT ≈ ΔIL / (8·fSW·COUT) + ΔIL·ESR Synchronous vs asynchronous: a low-side MOSFET (synchronous) replaces the Schottky diode and saves its 0.3–0.5 V drop, which matters a lot at low output v","g":"arch","lv":1},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"boost-bb","t":"Boost, buck-boost and inverting","s":"TopologyIdeal conversion ratioCharacteristicsTypical use BoostVOUT/VIN = 1/(1−D)Discontinuous output current, large output-cap ripple; right-half-plane zero (RHPZ) limits bandwidth; when off, input flows through the inductor and diode to th","g":"arch","lv":1},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"charge-pump","t":"Charge pumps (switched capacitor)","s":"A charge pump converts voltage with switches and capacitors only — no inductor. The ratio is fixed (×2, ×1.5, ÷2, −1) and efficiency is high when the ratio is \"just right\"; otherwise the excess voltage is lost much as in an LDO. 2:1 divider","g":"arch","lv":1},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"pmic","t":"PMIC: the whole power system in one chip","s":"A PMIC integrates multiple bucks, LDOs, boosts, load switches, power sequencing, monitoring ADCs, watchdogs, RTC, chargers and digital interfaces. It saves area and puts sequencing and monitoring in one place; the challenge is that switchin","g":"arch","lv":1},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"glossary","t":"Glossary","s":"","g":"arch","lv":1},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"glossary","t":"LDO","s":"Low-dropout linear regulator.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"glossary","t":"PMIC","s":"Power-management IC integrating multiple rails and management functions.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"glossary","t":"VDO","s":"Dropout voltage: the minimum input–output difference needed to regulate.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"glossary","t":"IQ","s":"Quiescent current.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"glossary","t":"Duty cycle D","s":"Fraction of the switching period the high-side switch is on.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"glossary","t":"CCM / DCM","s":"Continuous / discontinuous conduction mode of the inductor current.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"glossary","t":"PFM","s":"Pulse-frequency modulation: lowering switching frequency at light load for efficiency.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"glossary","t":"Shoot-through","s":"Both switches conducting at once, shorting VIN to ground.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"glossary","t":"RHPZ","s":"Right-half-plane zero in boost / buck-boost converters, limiting loop bandwidth.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"glossary","t":"DVS","s":"Dynamic voltage scaling.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"architecture.html","a":"glossary","t":"Post-regulation","s":"An LDO after a DC-DC, using the LDO's PSRR to remove switching ripple.","g":"arch","lv":3},{"p":"process.html","a":"bcd","t":"What BCD is","s":"In the mid-1980s ST (then SGS) introduced the first BCD process, putting bipolar transistors (analogue precision), CMOS (logic) and DMOS (power) on one wafer. Every foundry has since developed its own BCD platforms, shrinking from several µ","g":"fab","lv":1},{"p":"process.html","a":"device-menu","t":"The device menu","s":"CategoryDevicesUse in power ICs CMOS1.5 / 1.8 V core, 5 V I/O, HV CMOSDigital control, registers, OTP control, gate-driver front ends DMOSN-LDMOS, P-LDMOS (7–80 V), isolated LDMOSBuck high / low-side switches, LDO pass devices, load switche","g":"fab","lv":1},{"p":"process.html","a":"ldmos","t":"LDMOS: trading breakdown against on-resistance","s":"The key LDMOS metric is specific on-resistance Rsp = RDS(on) × area (mΩ·mm²). A longer, lighter drift region raises breakdown but also resistance; the silicon limit is roughly Rsp ∝ BV2.5. RESURF, multiple field plates and super-junctions a","g":"fab","lv":1},{"p":"process.html","a":"isolation","t":"Isolation and parasitics","s":"One P substrate carries power nodes switching to VIN, analogue circuits with tens-of-µV precision and GHz logic. They must be kept apart. IsolationHowProsCons Junction isolation (JI)N buried layer (NBL) + deep N-well around the device, reve","g":"fab","lv":1},{"p":"process.html","a":"substrate-injection","t":"Substrate injection: a side effect of the switch node","s":"During the buck dead time the inductor current freewheels through the low-side body diode, and SW drops to about −0.7 V. The N-type drain is then forward-biased to the P substrate and injects a large number of minority carriers (electrons),","g":"fab","lv":2},{"p":"process.html","a":"passives","t":"Resistors and capacitors","s":"DeviceSheet resistance / densityTemperature coefficientNotes P+ poly resistor100–300 Ω/□On the order of +100 ppm/°C (sign depends on doping)Common in feedback dividers; the ratio matters, so ±20% absolute error is fine High-resistance poly1","g":"fab","lv":1},{"p":"process.html","a":"metal","t":"Thick metal, on-resistance and electromigration","s":"In a 3 A buck with a 40 mΩ high side, on-chip metal and package bonding can add another 10–30 mΩ. Power processes therefore offer 3–6 µm thick top metal, or even a copper RDL over the wafer to carry current straight from the power device to","g":"fab","lv":1},{"p":"process.html","a":"trim-fuse","t":"Trimming: fuses, OTP and MTP","s":"Process variation can leave a bandgap with ±1–2% initial error and an oscillator ±10–20% off, while products need ±0.5–1% and ±2–3%. The answer is to measure, compute and then program a trim code at wafer test (CP) or after packaging (FT). ","g":"fab","lv":1},{"p":"process.html","a":"matching","t":"Matching and stress","s":"LDO accuracy comes mainly from offset in the differential pair and current mirrors, the divider ratio and the BJT area ratio in the bandgap — all questions of how identical two \"identical\" devices really are. σ(ΔVT) = AVT / √(W·L) σ(ΔR/R) =","g":"fab","lv":1},{"p":"process.html","a":"trends","t":"Process trends","s":"Finer BCD nodes: phone and DDR5 PMICs integrate lots of digital control, OTP and ADCs, pushing BCD to 55 / 40 nm while keeping 5 V LDMOS. GaN and SiC: 48 V server power, on-board chargers and fast chargers are adopting GaN HEMT switches wit","g":"fab","lv":1},{"p":"process.html","a":"glossary","t":"Glossary","s":"","g":"fab","lv":1},{"p":"process.html","a":"glossary","t":"BCD","s":"Bipolar-CMOS-DMOS integrated process.","g":"fab","lv":3},{"p":"process.html","a":"glossary","t":"LDMOS","s":"Laterally diffused MOS, the workhorse power device of power ICs.","g":"fab","lv":3},{"p":"process.html","a":"glossary","t":"Rsp","s":"Specific on-resistance, RDS(on) × area.","g":"fab","lv":3},{"p":"process.html","a":"glossary","t":"RESURF","s":"Reduced surface field: doping and field plates that spread the drift-region field evenly to raise breakdown.","g":"fab","lv":3},{"p":"process.html","a":"glossary","t":"NBL","s":"N-type buried layer, used for junction isolation and to lower parasitic PNP gain.","g":"fab","lv":3},{"p":"process.html","a":"glossary","t":"DTI","s":"Deep trench isolation.","g":"fab","lv":3},{"p":"process.html","a":"glossary","t":"Guard ring","s":"A ring around a device that collects injected carriers and lowers latch-up risk.","g":"fab","lv":3},{"p":"process.html","a":"glossary","t":"OTP","s":"One-time programmable non-volatile memory.","g":"fab","lv":3},{"p":"process.html","a":"glossary","t":"Piezoresistive effect","s":"Stress changing semiconductor resistance and carrier mobility.","g":"fab","lv":3},{"p":"process.html","a":"glossary","t":"SOA","s":"Safe operating area: the allowed combinations of voltage, current and time.","g":"fab","lv":3},{"p":"process.html","a":"glossary","t":"FOM","s":"Figure of merit, e.g. RDS(on)·Qg, comparing switching devices.","g":"fab","lv":3},{"p":"design.html","a":"ldo-loop","t":"The LDO loop: poles, zeros and phase margin","s":"Treat the LDO as a negative-feedback amplifier. Open-loop gain AOL = AEA · gm,pass · Rout · β, where β = R2/(R1+R2). Stability is decided by where a few poles and zeros sit: Pole / zeroLocationBehaviour Output pole pout1 / (2π · Rout · COUT","g":"design","lv":1},{"p":"design.html","a":"esr-window","t":"The ESR window and ceramic capacitors","s":"Early LDOs were designed around tantalum or aluminium electrolytic capacitors with 0.1 to several Ω of ESR, which supplied exactly the zero needed for stability. Datasheets drew a \"stable region of ESR vs load current\", nicknamed the tunnel","g":"design","lv":1},{"p":"design.html","a":"capless","t":"Capacitor-less LDOs and NMOS LDOs","s":"Digital LDOs inside an SoC or other on-chip LDOs have no room for µF capacitors — only hundreds of pF to a few nF on die. The output pole moves to high frequency and the dominant pole is put inside the EA (Miller compensation), at the cost ","g":"design","lv":1},{"p":"design.html","a":"psrr","t":"PSRR: keeping input ripple out","s":"PSRR = 20·log(ΔVIN/ΔVOUT). It is not one number but a curve against frequency, roughly in three regions: RegionDominant mechanismHow to improve Low frequency (< 1/10 of loop bandwidth)Loop gain: PSRR ≈ AOLMore EA gain, a better-rejecting re","g":"design","lv":1},{"p":"design.html","a":"noise","t":"Output noise","s":"Most LDO output noise comes from the reference and the EA input stage, then is amplified by the divider's (1 + R1/R2). So the same LDO is noisier at 3.3 V than at 1.2 V. NR / SS pin: an external capacitor after the reference forms an RC low","g":"design","lv":1},{"p":"design.html","a":"transient","t":"Load transient","s":"When load current jumps from 1 mA to 500 mA in 1 µs, the loop cannot react in time and the output capacitor supplies all of it: ΔVOUT ≈ ΔI · ESR + ΔI · tresp / COUT tresp ≈ 1/(2π·fUGB) + Cgate·ΔVgate/Islew Loop bandwidth: higher is faster, ","g":"design","lv":1},{"p":"design.html","a":"bandgap","t":"Bandgap references","s":"A BJT's VBE is about 0.6–0.7 V with a tempco of about −2 mV/°C (CTAT); the ΔVBE of two BJTs at different current densities, (kT/q)·ln(N), is proportional to absolute temperature (PTAT). Adding them in the right ratio cancels the tempco near","g":"design","lv":1},{"p":"design.html","a":"protection","t":"Protection circuits","s":"ProtectionHowDesign points / common traps Current limit (constant)Sense pass current (mirrored sense FET) and clamp the gate above a thresholdShort-circuit dissipation = VIN·ILIM, so thermal shutdown follows quickly Foldback limitThe lower ","g":"design","lv":1},{"p":"design.html","a":"dcdc-control","t":"DC-DC control modes","s":"Control modePrincipleProsCons Voltage mode (VM)Error voltage compared with a fixed rampNoise-immune, stable at light loadLC double pole needs Type III compensation; slower transient Peak current mode (PCM)Peak inductor current compared with","g":"design","lv":1},{"p":"design.html","a":"compensation","t":"Compensation networks","s":"Crossover frequency: usually fc ≤ fSW/10 (up to fSW/5 for current mode); boost / buck-boost also below 1/3–1/5 of the RHPZ. Type II (one zero, two poles): common with current mode; zero near the power-stage dominant pole, high-frequency pol","g":"design","lv":1},{"p":"design.html","a":"gate-drive","t":"Gate drive, dead time and bootstrap","s":"Bootstrap: an N-type high-side switch needs its gate VGS above SW; a capacitor charges while the low side is on and \"floats\" on SW when the high side is on. At 100% duty it cannot recharge, so the low side must be forced on periodically or ","g":"design","lv":1},{"p":"design.html","a":"pmic-digital","t":"The digital side of a PMIC","s":"Power-up / power-down sequencer: enables each rail in the order and with the delays set in OTP, waiting for the previous rail's PG. Power-down order matters just as much; getting it wrong can damage the processor or cause latch-up. Interfac","g":"design","lv":1},{"p":"design.html","a":"verification","t":"Design verification","s":"ItemContent PVT cornersProcess corners (TT / FF / SS / FS / SF) × voltage × temperature (−40 / 25 / 125 / 150 °C) Monte CarloOffset and accuracy before and after trim; estimate yield and check that the trim range is enough Stability sweepLo","g":"design","lv":1},{"p":"design.html","a":"glossary","t":"Glossary","s":"","g":"design","lv":1},{"p":"design.html","a":"glossary","t":"Phase margin (PM)","s":"How far the phase is from −180° where the gain falls to 0 dB.","g":"design","lv":3},{"p":"design.html","a":"glossary","t":"UGB","s":"Unity-gain bandwidth: the frequency where loop gain is 1, i.e. crossover.","g":"design","lv":3},{"p":"design.html","a":"glossary","t":"ESR / ESL","s":"A capacitor's equivalent series resistance / inductance.","g":"design","lv":3},{"p":"design.html","a":"glossary","t":"Miller compensation","s":"A capacitor across a gain stage that uses the Miller effect to move the dominant pole down and split the poles.","g":"design","lv":3},{"p":"design.html","a":"glossary","t":"CFF","s":"Feed-forward capacitor across the upper divider resistor.","g":"design","lv":3},{"p":"design.html","a":"glossary","t":"Foldback","s":"Current limit that falls as output voltage falls.","g":"design","lv":3},{"p":"design.html","a":"glossary","t":"Hiccup","s":"On a fault, shut down for a while and then retry.","g":"design","lv":3},{"p":"design.html","a":"glossary","t":"Slope compensation","s":"A ramp that prevents subharmonic oscillation in peak current mode above 50% duty.","g":"design","lv":3},{"p":"design.html","a":"glossary","t":"COT","s":"Constant on-time control.","g":"design","lv":3},{"p":"design.html","a":"glossary","t":"PTAT / CTAT","s":"Voltage or current proportional / complementary to absolute temperature.","g":"design","lv":3},{"p":"design.html","a":"glossary","t":"SPMI","s":"System Power Management Interface, the MIPI bus for mobile power management.","g":"design","lv":3},{"p":"design.html","a":"glossary","t":"FMEDA","s":"Failure Modes, Effects and Diagnostic Analysis, a functional-safety method.","g":"design","lv":3},{"p":"production.html","a":"flow","t":"The production flow","s":"","g":"mfg","lv":1},{"p":"production.html","a":"wat","t":"WAT: an electrical check-up before wafers ship","s":"Wafer acceptance test (WAT, also called PCM) measures basic device parameters on test keys in the scribe lines: VT, IDSAT, sheet resistance, contact resistance, BJT β, LDMOS Rsp and breakdown, capacitance, oxide breakdown. Acceptance: 5–9 s","g":"mfg","lv":1},{"p":"production.html","a":"cp-trim","t":"CP test and trim","s":"Wafer test (CP, chip probing) contacts each die's pads with a probe card. Power-IC CP has two special jobs: trimming, and measuring high-current parameters despite probe contact resistance. Opens / shorts and leakage confirm probe contact a","g":"mfg","lv":1},{"p":"production.html","a":"assembly","t":"Packaging","s":"PackageθJA (JEDEC board, magnitude)FeaturesTypical use SOT-23 / SC-70180–250 °C/WCheap, easy to solder and inspectLow-current LDOs, load switches SOIC / MSOP (with exposed pad)40–120 °C/WVisible leads, easy automotive inspectionAutomotive L","g":"mfg","lv":1},{"p":"production.html","a":"ft","t":"FT final test","s":"Final test (FT) after packaging runs on a handler and measures the guaranteed datasheet parameters. Unlike CP it can test high current, real switching and thermal behaviour, and catches packaging-induced problems. Temperature: consumer part","g":"mfg","lv":1},{"p":"production.html","a":"yield","t":"Yield","s":"Total yield Y = Yfab × YCP × Yassy × YFT YCP ≈ e−A·D0 × Yparam Defect yield: depends on die area and defect density. Power-IC dies are small (1–10 mm²), so defect yield is usually high. Parametric yield: often the main loss — trim range too","g":"mfg","lv":1},{"p":"production.html","a":"cpk","t":"Cpk and SPC","s":"Cp = (USL − LSL) / 6σ Cpk = min(USL − μ, μ − LSL) / 3σ CpkOut of spec (normal, one-sided)Usual reading 1.00≈ 1350 ppmInadequate 1.33≈ 32 ppmCommon consumer minimum 1.67≈ 0.3 ppmCommon automotive requirement (critical parameters) 2.00≈ 0.001","g":"mfg","lv":1},{"p":"production.html","a":"pat","t":"PAT and zero defects","s":"Automotive customers aim for < 1 DPPM, or zero defects. Spec limits alone are not enough, because a part that \"passes spec but sits far from its lot\" often carries a latent defect. Part average testing (PAT): per AEC-Q001, dynamic limits ar","g":"mfg","lv":1},{"p":"production.html","a":"traceability","t":"Traceability","s":"The first question in any complaint is \"which lot, which wafer, which position did this come from?\" The trace chain is usually: Package marking: part number + date code + lot code (small packages may only fit 3–4 characters). On-chip ECID: ","g":"mfg","lv":1},{"p":"production.html","a":"pcn","t":"PCN and change control","s":"Any change that may affect form, fit, function, quality or reliability must be notified to customers in advance (per JESD46, usually at least 90 days), with qualification data for major changes so customers can evaluate or requalify. Change","g":"mfg","lv":1},{"p":"production.html","a":"capacity","t":"Capacity, lead time and second sources","s":"8-inch fabs: a lot of power ICs still run on 8-inch BCD lines. In the 2020–2022 shortage, 8-inch capacity was one of the tightest bottlenecks and lead times stretched to 40–52 weeks or more. Second source: large customers want the same part","g":"mfg","lv":1},{"p":"production.html","a":"glossary","t":"Glossary","s":"","g":"mfg","lv":1},{"p":"production.html","a":"glossary","t":"WAT / PCM","s":"Wafer acceptance test / process control monitor.","g":"mfg","lv":3},{"p":"production.html","a":"glossary","t":"CP","s":"Chip probing, wafer-level test.","g":"mfg","lv":3},{"p":"production.html","a":"glossary","t":"FT","s":"Final test after packaging.","g":"mfg","lv":3},{"p":"production.html","a":"glossary","t":"Bin","s":"Test-result category; e.g. bin 1 is good, other bins are failure types.","g":"mfg","lv":3},{"p":"production.html","a":"glossary","t":"Guard band","s":"The margin by which test limits sit inside spec limits.","g":"mfg","lv":3},{"p":"production.html","a":"glossary","t":"OCAP","s":"Out-of-control action plan.","g":"mfg","lv":3},{"p":"production.html","a":"glossary","t":"PAT","s":"Part average testing: removing outliers based on the lot distribution.","g":"mfg","lv":3},{"p":"production.html","a":"glossary","t":"DPPM","s":"Defective parts per million.","g":"mfg","lv":3},{"p":"production.html","a":"glossary","t":"MSL","s":"Moisture sensitivity level.","g":"mfg","lv":3},{"p":"production.html","a":"glossary","t":"ECID","s":"Electronic chip ID: a unique identifier stored on the die.","g":"mfg","lv":3},{"p":"production.html","a":"glossary","t":"PCN / EOL / LTB","s":"Product change notice / end of life / last-time buy.","g":"mfg","lv":3},{"p":"test.html","a":"ldo-params","t":"How LDO parameters are measured","s":"ParameterMethodTraps Output accuracyFixed VIN and load; measure VOUTKelvin-sense at the pin, or trace drop is included Line regulationSweep VIN min to max; ΔVOUT/ΔVINMinimum VIN must stay out of dropout Load regulationLoad from min to max; ","g":"test","lv":1},{"p":"test.html","a":"dcdc-params","t":"DC-DC parameters","s":"Efficiency: η = POUT/PIN, with four-wire voltage sensing, precision shunts and thermal equilibrium at each load point. A 1% measurement error is enough to make efficiency curves \"look\" different. RDS(on): hold the high or low side on in a t","g":"test","lv":1},{"p":"test.html","a":"ate","t":"ATE production test","s":"A power-IC ATE (e.g. a mixed-signal tester) has several DPS (device power supplies), PMUs (precision measurement units), high-current / high-voltage sources, digital channels and digitisers. The core challenge of production test is measurin","g":"test","lv":1},{"p":"test.html","a":"dft","t":"Design for test (DFT)","s":"Analogue test bus: key internal nodes are muxed to a test pin or a functional pin. \"Trial trim\" in registers: write the trim code to a register first (without burning), measure, and only then program OTP — fewer programming errors. Protecti","g":"test","lv":1},{"p":"test.html","a":"kelvin","t":"Kelvin sensing and high current","s":"When testing a 30 mΩ switch, socket contact resistance alone may be 20–50 mΩ and rises with use. The answer is Kelvin (four-wire) measurement: one pair of force wires carries current, another pair of sense wires measures voltage at the pins","g":"test","lv":1},{"p":"test.html","a":"correlation","t":"Correlation and GR&R","s":"The same chip measured at CP, FT, on the bench and on the customer's line will not read exactly the same. Correlation proves that the differences are known and acceptable. Golden units: a set of samples precisely measured on the bench, used","g":"test","lv":1},{"p":"test.html","a":"bench","t":"Bench characterisation","s":"ItemMethod and equipment Parameters over temperatureThermostream or chamber, −40 to 150 °C, waiting for equilibrium at each point Loop Bode plotNetwork analyser + injection transformer / resistor in the feedback path; sweep load and capacit","g":"test","lv":1},{"p":"test.html","a":"qual","t":"Reliability qualification: AEC-Q100","s":"The automotive IC standard is AEC-Q100; consumer and industrial products are often based on JEDEC JESD47. The typical requirement is 3 lots × 77 units, zero failures, with pre/post parameter comparison. TestConditions (typical)Failure mecha","g":"test","lv":1},{"p":"test.html","a":"esd-lu","t":"ESD and latch-up","s":"ModelSimulatesTypical level HBM (human body model)A charged person touching a pin, 100 pF / 1.5 kΩ±2 kV (general), ±4 kV (common in automotive) CDM (charged device model)The charged chip itself touching ground through one pin; ns-scale high","g":"test","lv":1},{"p":"test.html","a":"safety","t":"Functional-safety testing","s":"Power-on self-test: monitor comparators, references and watchdog check themselves at every power-up; failure blocks release. Fault injection: switches designed into the monitor paths let production and the field verify that the monitors the","g":"test","lv":1},{"p":"test.html","a":"glossary","t":"Glossary","s":"","g":"test","lv":1},{"p":"test.html","a":"glossary","t":"ATE","s":"Automatic test equipment.","g":"test","lv":3},{"p":"test.html","a":"glossary","t":"DPS / PMU","s":"Device power supply / precision measurement unit on a tester.","g":"test","lv":3},{"p":"test.html","a":"glossary","t":"Load board","s":"The interface board between tester and device.","g":"test","lv":3},{"p":"test.html","a":"glossary","t":"Kelvin","s":"Four-wire measurement with separate force and sense.","g":"test","lv":3},{"p":"test.html","a":"glossary","t":"Golden unit","s":"A reference sample used to verify a measurement system.","g":"test","lv":3},{"p":"test.html","a":"glossary","t":"GR&R","s":"Gage repeatability and reproducibility of a measurement system.","g":"test","lv":3},{"p":"test.html","a":"glossary","t":"HTOL","s":"High-temperature operating life test.","g":"test","lv":3},{"p":"test.html","a":"glossary","t":"HAST","s":"Highly accelerated stress test (temperature and humidity).","g":"test","lv":3},{"p":"test.html","a":"glossary","t":"HBM / CDM","s":"Human body model / charged device model, two ESD models.","g":"test","lv":3},{"p":"test.html","a":"glossary","t":"Skew lot","s":"A qualification lot with process parameters deliberately at the corners.","g":"test","lv":3},{"p":"application.html","a":"power-tree","t":"The power tree","s":"Efficiency budget: battery power = Σ(load power / efficiency of that path). An LDO producing 1.8 V from the 1.9 V pre-regulator is ≈ 95% × buck efficiency; straight from a 4 V battery it is only 45%. Headroom margin: the pre-regulator must ","g":"app","lv":1},{"p":"application.html","a":"sequencing","t":"Power-up and power-down sequencing","s":"Most SoCs, FPGAs and DDR specify the order, slew and spacing of their rails. Violations range from failure to boot to I/Os back-feeding through ESD diodes and triggering latch-up. Common rules: core before I/O, I/O before analogue; or \"VIO ","g":"app","lv":1},{"p":"application.html","a":"ldo-or-dcdc","t":"LDO or DC-DC?","s":"ConditionLeans towardWhy Small VIN − VOUT (< 0.5 V), moderate currentLDOEfficiency close to DC-DC, fewer parts, no noise Large headroom, current > 200–300 mADC-DCThe LDO cannot handle the heat RF, PLL, ADC referenceLDO (or DC-DC + LDO)Low n","g":"app","lv":1},{"p":"application.html","a":"capacitors","t":"Capacitor selection: MLCC DC bias","s":"This is the most common trap in power applications. High-k MLCCs (X5R, X7R) lose a lot of capacitance under DC bias — more so in small cases and when the rated voltage is close to the working voltage. Condition (magnitude)Effective / nomina","g":"app","lv":1},{"p":"application.html","a":"inductor","t":"Inductor selection","s":"Inductance: usually chosen so ripple current ΔIL is 20–40% of maximum load. Too small means big ripple and loss; too big means slow transients and bulk. Current mode also needs slope compensation matched to the inductance. Saturation curren","g":"app","lv":1},{"p":"application.html","a":"thermal","t":"Thermal design","s":"TJ = TA + PD · θJA TJ ≈ Ttop + PD · ΨJT θJA depends on the board: a JEDEC 2s2p (4-layer) test board and a customer's small 2-layer board can differ by 1.5–2×. Exposed pad and vias: QFN / DFN pads need enough solder and thermal vias (e.g. 0.","g":"app","lv":1},{"p":"application.html","a":"layout","t":"PCB layout","s":"","g":"app","lv":1},{"p":"application.html","a":"layout-buck","t":"Buck layout","s":"Minimise the hot loop: the loop input cap → high side → low side → back to input cap carries pulsed current; its area sets parasitic inductance, SW ringing and EMI. The input capacitor must be closest to VIN and PGND, with the small high-fr","g":"app","lv":2},{"p":"application.html","a":"layout-ldo","t":"LDO layout","s":"Input and output capacitors tight to the pins, with short, wide ground returns to the GND pin. Route the remote-sense pin to the load to compensate long-trace drop. Return the NR / SS capacitor and FB divider grounds to the LDO's GND, not t","g":"app","lv":2},{"p":"application.html","a":"emi","t":"EMI and EMC","s":"Frequency choice: automotive avoids the AM band (530–1700 kHz), commonly using 400 kHz (below) or 2.1 MHz (above). Spread spectrum: dithering the switching frequency by ±5–10% lowers spectral peaks, but downstream ADCs may then see broadban","g":"app","lv":1},{"p":"application.html","a":"hot-plug","t":"Hot plug and input protection","s":"When a power cable or battery is plugged in, the cable inductance and a low-ESR ceramic capacitor form an LC resonance and the input can overshoot to nearly twice the supply voltage. Plugging in a 12 V adapter can show spikes above 20 V, ex","g":"app","lv":1},{"p":"application.html","a":"automotive","t":"Automotive power","s":"EventVoltage (12 V system, magnitude)Demands on the power IC Cold crankFalls to 3–6 V for tens of msBuck must run at 100% duty / low dropout, or use a buck-boost front end Start-stopRepeated dips to 6–8 VAs above, with no reset Load dumpUns","g":"app","lv":1},{"p":"application.html","a":"segments","t":"What each segment cares about","s":"SegmentFocus Phones / wearablesEfficiency (battery life), IQ, area and height, DVS speed, RF interference, audible noise AI / data centreHundreds to thousands of amps per processor, multiphase VRs, 48 V buses, transient di/dt, telemetry acc","g":"app","lv":1},{"p":"application.html","a":"selection","t":"Selection and design checklist","s":"Voltage and current: VIN range (including transients), VOUT, maximum and peak current, headroom margin. Thermal: worst-case PD; estimate TJ with the real board. Capacitors: confirm the minimum effective capacitance after DC bias, temperatur","g":"app","lv":1},{"p":"application.html","a":"glossary","t":"Glossary","s":"","g":"app","lv":1},{"p":"application.html","a":"glossary","t":"Power tree","s":"The conversion relationships between all rails in a system.","g":"app","lv":3},{"p":"application.html","a":"glossary","t":"DC bias","s":"The drop in MLCC capacitance with applied DC voltage.","g":"app","lv":3},{"p":"application.html","a":"glossary","t":"ΨJT","s":"Junction-to-top thermal characterisation parameter, used to infer junction temperature from case-top temperature.","g":"app","lv":3},{"p":"application.html","a":"glossary","t":"Hot loop","s":"The high-frequency loop in a buck through which pulsed current flows.","g":"app","lv":3},{"p":"application.html","a":"glossary","t":"Pre-bias","s":"Starting up with voltage already on the output.","g":"app","lv":3},{"p":"application.html","a":"glossary","t":"Spread spectrum","s":"Dithering the switching frequency to lower EMI peaks.","g":"app","lv":3},{"p":"application.html","a":"glossary","t":"Load dump","s":"The high-voltage transient from an automotive alternator when its load is suddenly disconnected.","g":"app","lv":3},{"p":"application.html","a":"glossary","t":"Cold crank","s":"The large battery-voltage dip when an engine starts.","g":"app","lv":3},{"p":"application.html","a":"glossary","t":"EOS","s":"Electrical overstress: damage from exceeding absolute maximum ratings.","g":"app","lv":3},{"p":"application.html","a":"glossary","t":"TVS","s":"Transient voltage suppressor diode.","g":"app","lv":3},{"p":"sic.html","a":"why-wbg","t":"Why wide bandgap","s":"The wider a semiconductor's bandgap, the higher the critical electric field it withstands. For the same voltage rating the drift region can be thinner and more heavily doped, so on-resistance falls dramatically; smaller devices also have sm","g":"sic","lv":1},{"p":"sic.html","a":"sic-vs-gan","t":"How SiC, GaN and Si divide the work","s":"Si super-junction MOSFETSiC MOSFETGaN HEMT Reverse recoveryLarge body-diode Qrr; unsuitable for hard-switched CCM totem-poleSmall body-diode Qrr; hard-switched totem-pole PFC worksNo body diode, Qrr ≈ 0, but high reverse-conduction drop Swi","g":"sic","lv":1},{"p":"sic.html","a":"dc-chain","t":"The data-center power chain","s":"From grid to GPU there are roughly five to seven conversion stages, and total efficiency is the product: ηtotal = ηUPS × ηPSU × ηIBC × ηVRM e.g. 0.97 × 0.975 × 0.975 × 0.90 ≈ 0.83 The VRM stage (0.8 V, thousands of amps) is usually the leas","g":"sic","lv":1},{"p":"sic.html","a":"psu","t":"Rack PSUs: which stage uses SiC","s":"A server PSU usually has two stages: power-factor correction (PFC) rectifies the AC and boosts it to about 400 V DC, then an isolated DC/DC (commonly an LLC resonant converter) steps down to a busbar of around 50 V. Bridgeless totem-pole PF","g":"sic","lv":1},{"p":"sic.html","a":"infineon","t":"Infineon as an example: products and reference designs","s":"ItemKey public information CoolSiC MOSFET 400 V (G2)Announced May 2024 for the AC/DC stage of AI server PSUs; ten parts in five RDS(on) classes from 11 to 45 mΩ, in TOLL and D²PAK-7 (Kelvin source, .XT interconnect). Infineon cites 99.5% ef","g":"sic","lv":1},{"p":"sic.html","a":"hvdc","t":"800 V HVDC and solid-state transformers","s":"Why high-voltage DC: above 100 kW per rack, a 54 V busbar carries thousands of amps; copper weight, drop and space become the bottleneck. At 800 V the current for the same power falls to about 1/15. Architecture change: AC-to-DC conversion ","g":"sic","lv":1},{"p":"sic.html","a":"design","t":"SiC design points","s":"TopicKey points Gate-drive voltageSiC has lower transconductance and needs a higher turn-on voltage (commonly +15 to +20 V, part dependent) to reach rated RDS(on); turn off at 0 V or negative (−2 to −5 V) to prevent false turn-on. Follow th","g":"sic","lv":1},{"p":"sic.html","a":"manufacturing","t":"Manufacturing: why SiC is expensive","s":"Substrates: SiC boules grow by sublimation (PVT) above 2000 °C — slowly and with low yield — so wafers cost far more than Si. The mainstream is moving from 150 mm to 200 mm to lower cost per die. Crystal defects: micropipes, basal plane dis","g":"sic","lv":1},{"p":"sic.html","a":"test-rel","t":"Test and reliability","s":"ItemWhat is special about SiC VT drift (BTI)Interface traps charge and discharge under gate bias, shifting VT; besides static HTGB, the industry runs dynamic gate-stress (AC switching) tests, and preconditioning and timing affect readings. ","g":"sic","lv":1},{"p":"sic.html","a":"issues","t":"Common problems and complaint scenarios","s":"SymptomLikely causeCheck / fix PFC 0.3–0.5% less efficient than the reference designGate drive too low (reused Si 12 V), no Kelvin source, dead time too long so the body diode conductsSet VGS per datasheet, measure real VGS waveforms, short","g":"sic","lv":1},{"p":"sic.html","a":"sources","t":"Sources","s":"EE Power: Infineon 12 kW PSU reference design Electronic Design: 12 kW high-density PSU reference design EE Power: CoolSiC MOSFET 400 V for AI servers Compound Semiconductor: Infineon 400 V CoolSiC Infineon whitepaper: SiC in data-center po","g":"sic","lv":1},{"p":"sic.html","a":"glossary","t":"Glossary","s":"","g":"sic","lv":1},{"p":"sic.html","a":"glossary","t":"Wide bandgap (WBG)","s":"Semiconductors with a bandgap clearly larger than silicon's, such as SiC and GaN.","g":"sic","lv":3},{"p":"sic.html","a":"glossary","t":"Totem-pole PFC","s":"Bridgeless totem-pole power-factor correction, a high-efficiency PFC topology without a rectifier bridge.","g":"sic","lv":3},{"p":"sic.html","a":"glossary","t":"LLC","s":"Inductor-inductor-capacitor resonant converter, common for soft-switched isolated DC/DC.","g":"sic","lv":3},{"p":"sic.html","a":"glossary","t":"Hold-up time","s":"How long the output stays in spec after input power is lost.","g":"sic","lv":3},{"p":"sic.html","a":"glossary","t":"HVDC","s":"High-voltage direct-current distribution.","g":"sic","lv":3},{"p":"sic.html","a":"glossary","t":"SST","s":"Solid-state transformer.","g":"sic","lv":3},{"p":"sic.html","a":"glossary","t":"IBC","s":"Intermediate bus converter (e.g. 48 V → 12 V).","g":"sic","lv":3},{"p":"sic.html","a":"glossary","t":"BPD","s":"Basal plane dislocation, the source of SiC bipolar degradation.","g":"sic","lv":3},{"p":"sic.html","a":"glossary","t":"SEB","s":"Single-event burnout caused by a particle strike.","g":"sic","lv":3},{"p":"sic.html","a":"glossary","t":"CMTI","s":"Common-mode transient immunity of an isolated driver.","g":"sic","lv":3},{"p":"sic.html","a":"glossary","t":"DPT","s":"Double-pulse test, the standard method for switching characterisation.","g":"sic","lv":3},{"p":"issues.html","a":"symptoms","t":"Symptom index","s":"SymptomMost common causes (by likelihood)Look at Output oscillates continuouslyToo little effective COUT (DC bias), ESR out of range, wrong CFF, high source impedanceOscillation Output periodically drops and recoversThermal-shutdown cycling","g":"issues","lv":1},{"p":"issues.html","a":"oscillation","t":"Oscillation","s":"SymptomLikely causeHow to checkFix LDO output oscillates sinusoidally (tens of kHz–MHz)Effective capacitance below minimum: MLCC DC bias, temperature, small 0402 caseCheck the capacitor's DC-bias curve; try a larger case or one more in para","g":"issues","lv":1},{"p":"issues.html","a":"startup","t":"Start-up failure and abnormal start-up","s":"SymptomLikely causeHow to checkFix No output at allEN floating or below threshold; VIN below UVLO; PMIC sequence waiting on a PGMeasure EN and VIN at the IC pins; read fault registersPull up / divide EN; check sequencing settings Output stu","g":"issues","lv":1},{"p":"issues.html","a":"overshoot","t":"Overshoot and undershoot","s":"SymptomLikely causeFix Overshoot at power-upSoft start too fast; EA saturates during start and overshoots on recovery; too little phase marginLarger soft-start capacitor; confirm stability Large undershoot on load riseToo little COUT; low l","g":"issues","lv":1},{"p":"issues.html","a":"thermal-shutdown","t":"Overheating and thermal shutdown","s":"Symptom: the output drops and recovers every tens of ms to seconds, the package is too hot to touch, and it goes away with less load or lower VIN. Calculate first: PD = (VIN − VOUT)·IOUT; estimate TJ with the real board's thermal resistance","g":"issues","lv":1},{"p":"issues.html","a":"noise-ripple","t":"Noise and ripple","s":"SymptomLikely causeFix Buck output shows hundreds of mV of spikesLong scope ground lead pickup (artefact); or ESL, layoutGround spring, measure across the capacitor; add a small high-frequency capacitor Buck ripple still visible after the L","g":"issues","lv":1},{"p":"issues.html","a":"efficiency","t":"Efficiency below expectation","s":"Measurement: is voltage four-wire measured at the IC pins? Are the meter shunt and lead drops included in PIN? Inductor: DCR too high, saturation, AC loss; compare with the datasheet's recommended inductor. Light load: is it in FPWM mode? (","g":"issues","lv":1},{"p":"issues.html","a":"reverse-current","t":"Reverse current and leakage","s":"Voltage remains after shutdown: another supply feeds this rail through I/O ESD diodes, or the load holds stored energy. Enable active discharge at shutdown, or avoid it in the system sequence. LDO input off, output held by a battery or big ","g":"issues","lv":1},{"p":"issues.html","a":"emi-issues","t":"EMI failures","s":"Conducted EMI (150 kHz–108 MHz): mainly the switching frequency and harmonics leaving through the input. Bigger input filter, spread spectrum, a different frequency. Radiated EMI (above 30 MHz): hot-loop area and SW ringing. Shrink the hot ","g":"issues","lv":1},{"p":"issues.html","a":"design-issues","t":"Design issues (on the chip)","s":"IssueSymptomRoot causePrevention Substrate injectionBad ADC readings at heavy load, comparator false triggers, random resetsMinority-carrier injection when SW goes below groundGuard rings, DTI, shorter dead time, layout isolation Start-up c","g":"issues","lv":1},{"p":"issues.html","a":"process-issues","t":"Process and package issues","s":"IssueSymptomRoot causeHow to catch it Package-stress shiftFT output mean shifted from CP, or varies with mould-compound lotMould shrinkage stress shifts bandgap and resistors via the piezoresistive effectPaired CP / FT analysis, post-packag","g":"issues","lv":1},{"p":"issues.html","a":"test-issues","t":"Test issues","s":"IssueSymptomRoot causeFix Socket wearA parameter distribution slowly shifts, yield drops, retest recovery highContact resistance risingGolden unit every shift, usage-count control, Kelvin Tester-to-tester differencesOne tester has systemati","g":"issues","lv":1},{"p":"issues.html","a":"debug-tips","t":"Measurement and debugging tips","s":"Confirm conditions: VIN (at the IC pins), load, temperature, EN and the actual external component values, compared with datasheet conditions. Measure correctly: ground spring or coax on the scope; 20 MHz bandwidth limit for ripple, full ban","g":"issues","lv":1},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"flow","t":"Flow and timelines","s":"Acknowledge (within 24 hours): confirm receipt, open a complaint number, assign an owner. Initial containment (24–72 hours): decide whether to freeze inventory, screen goods in transit and notify other customers on the same lots. Gather inf","g":"complaints","lv":1},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"info","t":"Information to collect from the customer","s":"CategoryQuestions IdentificationFull part number, marking (date code, lot code), purchase channel (direct, distributor, open market) QuantityFailures / total used, and at which stage (incoming inspection, after SMT, functional test, burn-in","g":"complaints","lv":1},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"fa","t":"The failure-analysis flow","s":"Visual inspection: optical microscope for package cracks, burns, damaged leads, solder residue and correct marking (counterfeit check). X-ray: broken or displaced bond wires, solder voids, die cracks. C-SAM acoustic scan: delamination and v","g":"complaints","lv":1},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"classification","t":"Failure classification","s":"ClassTypical FA findingOwnership and direction EOS (electrical overstress)Large areas of melted metal, burned-through power devices, charred packageMostly application transients; find the energy source ESDSmall melt spots in ESD structures ","g":"complaints","lv":1},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"eight-d","t":"The 8D report","s":"StepContentCommon problems D0 PrepareDecide whether an 8D is needed; emergency response D1 TeamCross-functional: quality, product, test, design, applications, manufacturingQuality writes it alone D2 Problem description5W2H: what, where, whe","g":"complaints","lv":1},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"tools","t":"Root-cause tools","s":"5 Whys: keep asking \"why\" until you reach a system cause you can change, not \"operator error\". Fishbone (Ishikawa): list possible causes by man, machine, material, method, environment and measurement (6M) so nothing is missed. Is / is-not a","g":"complaints","lv":1},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"cases","t":"Typical complaint cases","s":"","g":"complaints","lv":1},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"c-eos","t":"Hot-plug spike destroys a buck input","s":"EOSConsumer · 12 V adapter SymptomProducts occasionally fail to power on at end users; returned buck ICs have VIN shorted to ground, at about 0.1% failure rate. AnalysisFA shows a large melted area in the high-side switch — classic EOS. Plu","g":"complaints","lv":2},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"c-mlcc","t":"New capacitor supplier, and the LDO starts oscillating","s":"ApplicationIndustrial · 3.3 V LDO SymptomAfter two trouble-free years in production, 3% of boards in one month show bad ADC readings in functional test; the customer suspects an LDO lot problem. AnalysisAll returned LDOs pass (NTF). The fai","g":"complaints","lv":2},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"c-otp","t":"Wrong OTP setting, wrong output voltage","s":"ProductionPMIC · part configuration SymptomA customer receives a reel of PMICs where one rail outputs 1.8 V instead of 1.2 V, damaging the SoC. AnalysisReading out the OTP shows the settings of another part number built on the same die. Tra","g":"complaints","lv":2},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"c-escape","t":"Cold UVLO threshold escapes test","s":"TestAutomotive · LDO SymptomOn winter cold starts, ECUs in a few vehicles reset. Returned LDOs work at room temperature. AnalysisAt −40 °C, the failing units' rising UVLO threshold is 150 mV above spec; others from the lot are fine. CP data","g":"complaints","lv":2},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"c-delam","t":"MSL violation causes delamination","s":"PackageConsumer · QFN PMIC SymptomPost-SMT functional-test fallout suddenly rises to 0.5%, with output shifts and intermittent opens. AnalysisC-SAM shows delamination between die surface and mould compound; unmounted parts from the same lot","g":"complaints","lv":2},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"c-ntf","t":"All returns are good: a soldering problem","s":"NTFWearable · WLCSP LDO SymptomAfter drop testing, some products show output faults; the customer returns 20 LDOs for analysis. AnalysisAll 20 pass ATE and bench tests. A failing board is requested; a cross-section shows cracks between the ","g":"complaints","lv":2},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"c-pcn","t":"An unannounced mould-compound change","s":"ChangeIndustrial · precision LDO SymptomIn the customer's hot-humid ageing test, LDO output drifts 0.8%, beyond the system's 0.5% allowance; older lots do not. AnalysisComparing lots shows the assembly site switched to an equivalent-grade m","g":"complaints","lv":2},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"c-esd","t":"System ESD testing kills the IC","s":"ESDConsumer · USB port power switch SymptomAfter IEC 61000-4-2 ±8 kV contact discharge, the load switch fails; the customer says the datasheet's ±2 kV HBM is not enough. AnalysisFA shows the input ESD structure melted. The IC meets its HBM ","g":"complaints","lv":2},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"c-counterfeit","t":"Counterfeits bought on the open market","s":"CounterfeitDuring a shortage · LDO SymptomDuring a shortage the customer buys from an unauthorised channel; the new lot has high IQ, large dropout and some units are unstable. AnalysisMarking font differs from the original, date and lot cod","g":"complaints","lv":2},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"c-foldback","t":"Foldback current limit stops an LDO starting","s":"DesignIndustrial · FPGA auxiliary rail SymptomOn some boards the output sticks at 0.6 V after power-up instead of reaching 2.5 V; re-powering sometimes helps. AnalysisThe FPGA auxiliary rail starts drawing heavily at low voltage; the LDO's ","g":"complaints","lv":2},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"communication","t":"Talking to the customer","s":"Respond first, analyse second: an initial reply within 24 hours stating receipt, next steps and timeline beats a full report after three days of silence. Let data speak: \"the IC is fine\" needs test data, swap results and waveforms, not just","g":"complaints","lv":1},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"metrics","t":"Quality metrics","s":"MetricDefinitionTypical target DPPMConfirmed customer rejects / units shipped × 106Consumer < 10–50; automotive < 1 First response timeFrom complaint to first reply< 24 hours 8D cycle timeFrom complaint to 8D closure< 30 days FA cycle timeF","g":"complaints","lv":1},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"glossary","t":"Glossary","s":"","g":"complaints","lv":1},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"glossary","t":"8D","s":"Eight Disciplines, an eight-step problem-solving and reporting format.","g":"complaints","lv":3},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"glossary","t":"FA","s":"Failure analysis.","g":"complaints","lv":3},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"glossary","t":"NTF / NFF","s":"No trouble found / no fault found.","g":"complaints","lv":3},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"glossary","t":"RMA","s":"Return material authorisation.","g":"complaints","lv":3},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"glossary","t":"C-SAM","s":"C-mode scanning acoustic microscopy, for package delamination.","g":"complaints","lv":3},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"glossary","t":"EMMI","s":"Emission microscopy, detecting faint light from defects.","g":"complaints","lv":3},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"glossary","t":"OBIRCH","s":"Optical beam induced resistance change, a laser-heating localisation method.","g":"complaints","lv":3},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"glossary","t":"FIB","s":"Focused ion beam, for cross-sections and circuit edits.","g":"complaints","lv":3},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"glossary","t":"Containment","s":"Short-term measures that stop suspect parts reaching customers.","g":"complaints","lv":3},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"glossary","t":"Escape","s":"A defective part that passes test and reaches the customer.","g":"complaints","lv":3},{"p":"complaints.html","a":"glossary","t":"FMEA","s":"Failure mode and effects analysis.","g":"complaints","lv":3},{"p":"roles.html","a":"overview","t":"Overview","s":"RoleIn one lineMost relevant chapters Analogue designDesigns LDOs, DC-DCs, references and protection; owns spec and stabilityArchitecture, design, process Analogue layoutTurns circuits into masks; handles matching, power paths, isolation an","g":"roles","lv":1},{"p":"roles.html","a":"analog","t":"Analogue design","s":"Analogue designers start from the spec and choose an architecture (PMOS / NMOS LDO, control mode), design the error amplifier, compensation, reference, protection and bias, run PVT and Monte Carlo, and lead characterisation and debug when s","g":"roles","lv":1},{"p":"roles.html","a":"analog-q","t":"Typical interview questions","s":"Where is the dominant pole of a PMOS LDO? How does it move with load, and why is light load usually more stable? Why do old LDOs oscillate with ceramic capacitors, and how does internal compensation fix it? What sets PSRR at low, mid and hi","g":"roles","lv":2},{"p":"roles.html","a":"layout","t":"Analogue layout","s":"Layout engineers make the circuit physical: metal and via plans for large power devices, common-centroid matching for precision devices, isolation between sensitive circuits and power stages, ESD and guard rings, electromigration and IR-dro","g":"roles","lv":1},{"p":"roles.html","a":"layout-q","t":"Typical interview questions","s":"What does a common-centroid layout cancel? Why add dummy devices? How should metal be distributed over a power device for even current? How do you decide via counts? What does a guard ring do? Which carriers do N+ and P+ rings collect? Wher","g":"roles","lv":2},{"p":"roles.html","a":"digital","t":"System / digital design","s":"The digital side of a PMIC includes the power sequencer, I²C / SPMI / PMBus interfaces, registers, OTP control, fault state machines, watchdogs and functional-safety mechanisms. Digital designers also run mixed-signal verification with the ","g":"roles","lv":1},{"p":"roles.html","a":"digital-q","t":"Typical interview questions","s":"Design a four-rail power-up sequencer: what states does it need, and how is power-down handled? How do you detect OTP read errors, and how do you make programming mistake-proof? On a fault, should you shut one rail, all rails, or hiccup? Wh","g":"roles","lv":2},{"p":"roles.html","a":"ae","t":"Applications engineering AE / FAE","s":"AEs write application notes and reference designs, build evaluation boards and characterisation data; FAEs support design-ins at customers, review schematics and layouts, and handle field problems. In a complaint, AE / FAE is usually the fi","g":"roles","lv":1},{"p":"roles.html","a":"ae-q","t":"Typical interview questions","s":"A customer says the LDO oscillates. What do you ask first, and how do you measure on site? How much is left of a 10 µF / 0603 / 6.3 V capacitor at 3.3 V? How do you find out? 5 V to 3.3 V at 500 mA — is a SOT-23 OK? How do you work it out? ","g":"roles","lv":2},{"p":"roles.html","a":"pe","t":"Product engineering (PE)","s":"A PE \"owns\" a product after it enters production: monitoring CP / FT yield and trim-code distributions, leading yield improvement, Cpk improvement and test-cost reduction; evaluating process or package changes and PCNs; and supplying lot da","g":"roles","lv":1},{"p":"roles.html","a":"pe-q","t":"Typical interview questions","s":"What out-of-spec rates do Cpk 1.33 and 1.67 represent? Why is a post-trim uniform distribution unsuitable for direct Cpk? Yield dropped 2% this week. How do you chase it, and what data do you look at? The trim-code distribution is drifting ","g":"roles","lv":2},{"p":"roles.html","a":"te","t":"Test engineering (TE)","s":"TEs develop and maintain ATE programs, load boards and probe cards / sockets, set test limits and guard bands, optimise test time and multi-site efficiency, and own correlation between testers and between CP, FT and the bench. Read: how LDO","g":"roles","lv":1},{"p":"roles.html","a":"te-q","t":"Typical interview questions","s":"How do you measure a 30 mΩ switch at CP, and how do you exclude contact resistance? With a large capacitor on the LDO output, how do you shorten test time? What does GR&R mean and what is acceptable? Yield is low but most units pass on rete","g":"roles","lv":2},{"p":"roles.html","a":"pi","t":"Process integration / device engineering","s":"At an IDM or foundry, this role develops and stabilises the BCD platform's devices (LDMOS, BJTs, resistors, capacitors, OTP); at a fabless company it is the foundry interface, owning WAT monitoring, process-change evaluation and wafer-side ","g":"roles","lv":1},{"p":"roles.html","a":"pi-q","t":"Typical interview questions","s":"How do you trade LDMOS Rsp against breakdown? What is RESURF? Where does LDMOS hot-carrier degradation happen, and how is it qualified? Pros and cons of junction isolation, DTI and SOI? High-resistance poly resistors drift after packaging. ","g":"roles","lv":2},{"p":"roles.html","a":"qra","t":"Quality and reliability (QRA)","s":"QRA plans and runs qualification, manages PCNs and changes, maintains outgoing-quality metrics, and leads complaint handling and 8D reports. For automotive products it also handles PPAP, IATF 16949 audits and functional-safety documentation","g":"roles","lv":1},{"p":"roles.html","a":"qra-q","t":"Typical interview questions","s":"What are AEC-Q100's main tests and sample sizes? How do Grade 1 and Grade 0 differ? Can an IC rated ±2 kV HBM survive system-level ±8 kV? Why or why not? Why must 8D step D4 find both occurrence and escape root causes? The customer's return","g":"roles","lv":2},{"p":"roles.html","a":"fa","t":"Failure analysis (FA)","s":"FA engineers run non-destructive and destructive analysis on returns: X-ray, C-SAM, curve tracing, decapsulation, hot-spot localisation, delayering, FIB and SEM. Good FA does not just find \"where it burned\" but infers where the energy came ","g":"roles","lv":1},{"p":"roles.html","a":"fa-q","t":"Typical interview questions","s":"How do EOS and ESD differ in FA signatures? Why do non-destructive analysis first, and in what order? What defects are EMMI and OBIRCH each good at finding? C-SAM shows delamination. How do you decide whether it caused the failure? Titles a","g":"roles","lv":2},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"cat-basic","t":"Devices and basics","s":"","g":"glossary","lv":1},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ac","t":"AC Alternating current","s":"Electricity whose direction alternates periodically; mains is 50/60 Hz AC. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ac-dc","t":"AC-DC AC-DC conversion","s":"Turning mains AC into DC, e.g. chargers and the front end of server PSUs. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-bandgap-reference","t":"Bandgap reference Bandgap reference","s":"Adds the falling V_BE and the rising ΔV_BE to get a ~1.2 V reference that barely changes with temperature. Also: bandgap reference, bandgap references Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-bcd","t":"BCD","s":"Bipolar-CMOS-DMOS integrated process. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-bjt","t":"BJT Bipolar junction transistor","s":"Base current controls collector current; used in bandgaps and precision analogue. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-boost","t":"Boost Boost / buck-boost converter","s":"A boost makes the output higher than the input; a buck-boost can go either way, for batteries that cross the output voltage. Also: Buck-Boost Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-bti","t":"BTI Bias temperature instability","s":"Gate bias plus heat slowly shifts V_T; NBTI is the negative-bias PMOS case, also prominent in SiC. Also: NBTI Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-buck","t":"Buck Buck converter","s":"A DC-DC that steps voltage down with a switch and inductor; ideally V_OUT = duty × V_IN, 85–95% efficient. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-bv","t":"BV Breakdown voltage","s":"The voltage at which a device breaks down and conducts heavily; sets its rating. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-cmos","t":"CMOS Complementary MOS","s":"Circuits built from PMOS and NMOS pairs; the mainstream for digital logic. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-dc-dc","t":"DC-DC DC-DC converter","s":"A switching converter from one DC voltage to another, e.g. buck or boost. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-dmos","t":"DMOS Double-diffused MOS","s":"A power MOS whose channel is formed by two diffusions; LDMOS is the lateral version. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-dti","t":"DTI","s":"Deep trench isolation. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-eeprom","t":"EEPROM Electrically erasable PROM / multi-time programmable","s":"Keeps data without power and can be rewritten; stores trim or configuration. Also: MTP Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-em","t":"EM Electromigration","s":"High current density pushes metal atoms along, forming voids or hillocks that open or short lines. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-fom","t":"FOM","s":"Figure of merit, e.g. RDS(on)·Qg, comparing switching devices. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-guard-ring","t":"Guard ring","s":"A ring around a device that collects injected carriers and lowers latch-up risk. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-hemt","t":"HEMT High-electron-mobility transistor","s":"The GaN power-device structure, conducting through a 2D electron gas at a heterojunction; very fast. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-hv","t":"HV High voltage","s":"Device variants rated for higher voltage, e.g. HV CMOS. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ic","t":"IC Integrated circuit","s":"A circuit with transistors, resistors and capacitors built on one silicon die. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-igbt","t":"IGBT Insulated-gate bipolar transistor","s":"A MOS-gated bipolar power device for high voltage and current, e.g. motor drives and inverters. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ji","t":"JI Junction isolation","s":"Isolating devices from the substrate with reverse-biased PN junctions; cheap but parasitic. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ldmos","t":"LDMOS","s":"Laterally diffused MOS, the workhorse power device of power ICs. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ldo","t":"LDO Low-dropout regulator","s":"A linear regulator whose pass device acts as a variable resistor and needs only a small input-output difference. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-mim","t":"MIM MIM / MOM / PIP capacitors","s":"On-chip capacitor structures: metal-insulator-metal, metal-oxide-metal (finger), poly-insulator-poly. Also: MOM, PIP Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-mos","t":"MOS Metal-oxide-semiconductor","s":"Short for MOSFET, or the three-layer structure itself (e.g. a MOS capacitor). Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-mosfet","t":"MOSFET Metal-oxide-semiconductor field-effect transistor","s":"A transistor whose gate voltage controls drain-source current; the main switch in power ICs. Also: FET Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-nbl","t":"NBL","s":"N-type buried layer, used for junction isolation and to lower parasitic PNP gain. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-nmos","t":"NMOS N-channel MOSFET","s":"Turns on when the gate is above the source; lower on-resistance than a same-size PMOS, low output impedance as a source follower. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-npn","t":"NPN NPN / PNP transistors","s":"The two BJT polarities; early regulators used an NPN Darlington or a PNP as pass device. Also: PNP Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-otp","t":"OTP","s":"One-time programmable non-volatile memory. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-piezoresistive-effect","t":"Piezoresistive effect","s":"Stress changing semiconductor resistance and carrier mobility. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-pmic","t":"PMIC Power-management IC","s":"One chip integrating several bucks, LDOs, sequencing, monitoring and interfaces. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-pmos","t":"PMOS P-channel MOSFET","s":"Turns on when the gate is below the source; the usual LDO pass device, needing no drive above V_IN. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-pn","t":"PN PN junction","s":"The boundary between P- and N-type silicon, i.e. a diode; reverse-biased it isolates. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-rdl","t":"RDL Redistribution layer","s":"An extra metal layer (often copper) above the die that routes current or signals to pads or bumps. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-resurf","t":"RESURF","s":"Reduced surface field: doping and field plates that spread the drift-region field evenly to raise breakdown. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-rsp","t":"Rsp","s":"Specific on-resistance, RDS(on) × area. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-scr","t":"SCR Silicon-controlled rectifier (PNPN)","s":"Once triggered it conducts until power is removed; parasitic PNPN structures cause latch-up. Also: PNPN Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-sicr","t":"SiCr Silicon-chromium / tantalum-nitride thin film","s":"Low-tempco thin-film resistor materials for precision references; laser-trimmable. Also: TaN Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-soa","t":"SOA","s":"Safe operating area: the allowed combinations of voltage, current and time. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-soi","t":"SOI Silicon on insulator","s":"Devices sit on a buried oxide: full dielectric isolation, no substrate injection, poorer heat flow. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-spice","t":"SPICE Circuit simulator","s":"A tool that solves circuit equations with device models; the standard for analogue design. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-tddb","t":"TDDB Time-dependent dielectric breakdown","s":"Oxide damage accumulates under field until it breaks down; used to project gate-oxide life. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"cat-ckt","t":"Circuits, control and interfaces","s":"","g":"glossary","lv":1},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-adc","t":"ADC Analogue-to-digital converter","s":"Converts an analogue voltage to a number; very sensitive to supply noise. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-avs","t":"AVS Adaptive voltage scaling","s":"Adjusting supply voltage to the chip's actual speed and temperature to save more power. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-bist","t":"BIST Built-in self-test","s":"The chip checks its own monitors and references; common in functional safety. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-boot","t":"BOOT Bootstrap pin","s":"Holds the bootstrap capacitor so an N-type high side can be driven above SW; a series resistor slows switching to cut EMI. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ccm-dcm","t":"CCM / DCM","s":"Continuous / discontinuous conduction mode of the inductor current. Also: CCM, DCM Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-cff","t":"CFF","s":"Feed-forward capacitor across the upper divider resistor. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-cot","t":"COT","s":"Constant on-time control. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-crc","t":"CRC Cyclic redundancy check","s":"A checksum to detect read errors, e.g. verifying OTP contents. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-current-limit-short-circuit-current","t":"Current limit / short-circuit current","s":"Maximum current into a short; may be constant-current or foldback. Also: Current limit, short-circuit current Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-dcr","t":"DCR DC resistance","s":"The DC resistance of an inductor winding; sets copper loss. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-dldo","t":"DLDO Digital LDO","s":"An LDO that replaces the analogue EA with digitally controlled switch arrays; works at very low voltage. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-duty-cycle-d","t":"Duty cycle D","s":"Fraction of the switching period the high-side switch is on. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-dvs","t":"DVS","s":"Dynamic voltage scaling. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ea","t":"EA Error amplifier","s":"Compares the feedback with the reference and amplifies the difference to drive the pass device or PWM. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-en","t":"EN Enable pin","s":"Pull high to turn on, low to turn off; leaving it floating can half-enable the part. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-esr-esl","t":"ESR / ESL","s":"A capacitor's equivalent series resistance / inductance. Also: ESL, ESR Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-fb","t":"FB Feedback pin / node","s":"The divided-down output goes here to be compared with the reference; sets the output voltage. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-fmeda","t":"FMEDA","s":"Failure Modes, Effects and Diagnostic Analysis, a functional-safety method. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-foldback","t":"Foldback","s":"Current limit that falls as output voltage falls. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-fpwm","t":"FPWM Forced PWM","s":"Fixed-frequency PWM even at light load: predictable ripple, poor efficiency, inductor current may go negative. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-fvf","t":"FVF Flipped voltage follower","s":"A fast, low-output-impedance local feedback cell used in capacitor-less LDOs. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-gnd","t":"GND Ground","s":"GND is ground; PGND carries power return current, AGND is the quiet analogue reference, joined at one point. Also: PGND, AGND Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ground-current-ignd","t":"Ground current IGND","s":"Current to ground under load; close to IQ for PMOS LDOs, rising with load for PNP LDOs. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-hiccup","t":"Hiccup","s":"On a fault, shut down for a while and then retry. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-iq","t":"IQ","s":"Quiescent current. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ir","t":"IR IR drop","s":"Voltage lost as current I flows through resistance R, e.g. in on-chip metal or PCB copper. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-i-c","t":"I²C Inter-integrated circuit bus","s":"A two-wire clock-and-data interface for reading and writing registers, the most common PMIC interface; SMBus and PMBus build on it. Also: SMBus Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-lc","t":"LC Inductor-capacitor network","s":"A filter or resonant circuit of an inductor and capacitor; a buck output filter is an LC. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-lsb","t":"LSB Least significant bit","s":"The smallest step of a digital code; in trimming, the change per step. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-miller-compensation","t":"Miller compensation","s":"A capacitor across a gain stage that uses the Miller effect to move the dominant pole down and split the poles. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-mlcc","t":"MLCC Multilayer ceramic capacitor","s":"The most common SMD capacitor; high-k types lose much capacitance to DC bias, temperature and ageing. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-nr","t":"NR Noise-reduction pin","s":"A capacitor here low-pass filters the reference to cut LDO output noise. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-output-accuracy","t":"Output accuracy","s":"The sum of reference, divider, EA offset, temperature, line and load regulation; typically ±1% (25 °C) or ±2% (full temperature). Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-output-noise","t":"Output noise","s":"Integrated noise from 10 Hz to 100 kHz, in µVRMS. Low-noise LDOs reach < 5 µVRMS. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-pfc","t":"PFC Power-factor correction","s":"Makes AC input current follow the voltage waveform to cut harmonics; the front stage of a server PSU, boosting to about 400 V. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-pfm","t":"PFM","s":"Pulse-frequency modulation: lowering switching frequency at light load for efficiency. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-pg","t":"PG Power good","s":"A status signal released once the output is in range, usually open-drain; can drive the next rail's EN for sequencing. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-phase-margin-pm","t":"Phase margin (PM)","s":"How far the phase is from −180° where the gain falls to 0 dB. Also: PM, Phase margin Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-pll","t":"PLL Phase-locked loop / voltage-controlled oscillator","s":"Circuits that generate clocks; a VCO's frequency follows a voltage, so supply noise becomes phase noise. Also: VCO Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-pmbus","t":"PMBus Power Management Bus","s":"A power-specific command set on SMBus / I²C, common in server power. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-post-regulation","t":"Post-regulation","s":"An LDO after a DC-DC, using the LDO's PSRR to remove switching ripple. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-psrr","t":"PSRR","s":"How much input ripple is rejected, in dB, falling with frequency. See Design · PSRR. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ptat-ctat","t":"PTAT / CTAT","s":"Voltage or current proportional / complementary to absolute temperature. Also: CTAT, PTAT Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-pwm","t":"PWM Pulse-width modulation","s":"Fixed frequency with a varying on-time fraction (duty cycle) to control the output. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-quiescent-current-iq","t":"Quiescent current IQ","s":"Current the LDO draws itself at no load. Wearables and IoT want < 1 µA; high-PSRR, high-bandwidth LDOs may need tens to hundreds of µA. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-rc","t":"RC Resistor-capacitor network","s":"A resistor-capacitor filter, delay or damping circuit. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-rhpz","t":"RHPZ","s":"Right-half-plane zero in boost / buck-boost converters, limiting loop bandwidth. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-rtc","t":"RTC Real-time clock","s":"A clock that keeps time on a backup battery; often integrated in PMICs. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-shoot-through","t":"Shoot-through","s":"Both switches conducting at once, shorting VIN to ground. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-shutdown-current-isd","t":"Shutdown current ISD","s":"Leakage with EN low; sets standby battery life, typically < 100 nA. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-slope-compensation","t":"Slope compensation","s":"A ramp that prevents subharmonic oscillation in peak current mode above 50% duty. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-spi","t":"SPI Serial peripheral interface","s":"A four-wire synchronous serial interface, faster than I²C. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-spmi","t":"SPMI","s":"System Power Management Interface, the MIPI bus for mobile power management. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ss","t":"SS Soft-start pin","s":"Sets how fast the output ramps, limiting inrush; in process corners SS means slow-slow. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-stable-capacitor-range","t":"Stable capacitor range","s":"The minimum COUT and ESR range in the datasheet. Outside it the LDO may oscillate — the most common application problem. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-svid","t":"SVID Processor voltage-control interfaces","s":"SVID (Intel) and SVI3 (AMD) let the processor command its VR to change core voltage on the fly. Also: SVI3 Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-sw","t":"SW Switch node","s":"The node between the high- and low-side switches of a buck, at the inductor; swings fast between V_IN and 0. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-type-ii","t":"Type II Type II / Type III compensation","s":"DC-DC compensation networks: Type II has one zero and two poles (current mode), Type III two zeros and three poles (voltage mode, for the LC double pole). Also: Type III Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ugb","t":"UGB","s":"Unity-gain bandwidth: the frequency where loop gain is 1, i.e. crossover. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-vdd","t":"VDD Supply-rail names","s":"VDD is the main supply; in DDR, VDDQ feeds I/O and VPP the word-line boost. Also: VDDQ, VPP Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-vdo","t":"VDO","s":"Dropout voltage: the minimum input–output difference needed to regulate. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-vm","t":"VM Voltage-mode control","s":"DC-DC control that compares the error voltage with a fixed ramp to make PWM. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-vrm","t":"VRM Voltage regulator module","s":"The multiphase buck feeding a processor or GPU core, up to thousands of amps. Also: VR Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-x5r","t":"X5R MLCC dielectric classes","s":"X5R (−55~85 °C ±15%), X7R (−55~125 °C ±15%), Y5V (huge variation, avoid for power), C0G / NP0 (stable but small values). Also: X7R, Y5V, C0G, NP0 Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"cat-prot","t":"Protection and sequencing","s":"","g":"glossary","lv":1},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-desat","t":"DESAT Desaturation detection","s":"Detects an abnormal rise in on-state voltage (a short or overcurrent) and turns the switch off at once. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ocp","t":"OCP Overcurrent protection / Open Compute Project","s":"In circuits, overcurrent protection; in data centers (OCP Open Rack), the Open Compute Project that publishes open server and rack specs. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ovp","t":"OVP Overvoltage protection","s":"Shuts down when input or output voltage is too high, protecting chip and load. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-sscb","t":"SSCB Solid-state circuit breaker","s":"A breaker using semiconductor switches instead of contacts; cuts DC fault current fast. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-tsd","t":"TSD Thermal shutdown","s":"Turns the output off when the die exceeds a threshold (typically 150–170 °C) and back on after cooling. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-uvlo","t":"UVLO Undervoltage lockout","s":"Turns the output off when the input falls below a threshold and back on only above a higher one (hysteresis), avoiding misbehaviour and restart loops at low voltage. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-uvp","t":"UVP Undervoltage protection","s":"Trips when the output falls too low (e.g. a short), often with hiccup restart. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"cat-mfg","t":"Process, packaging and production","s":"","g":"glossary","lv":1},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-aoi","t":"AOI Automated optical inspection","s":"Cameras automatically inspect solder joints and placement; QFNs need wettable flanks for visible joints. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-bin","t":"Bin","s":"Test-result category; e.g. bin 1 is good, other bins are failure types. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-bom","t":"BOM Bill of materials","s":"All part numbers and quantities in a product; part changes are a frequent complaint trigger. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-cp","t":"CP","s":"Chip probing, wafer-level test. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-cpk","t":"Cpk Process capability index","s":"Cpk = distance from the mean to the nearest spec limit ÷ 3σ; 1.33 is about 32 ppm out of spec, 1.67 about 0.3 ppm. Also: Cp Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-dft","t":"DFT Design for test","s":"Building in test modes, an analogue test bus and scan chains to make production test possible. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-dppm","t":"DPPM","s":"Defective parts per million. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ecid","t":"ECID","s":"Electronic chip ID: a unique identifier stored on the die. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ep","t":"EP Exposed pad","s":"A metal pad under the package, soldered to the PCB to conduct heat away. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-fcqfn","t":"FCQFN Flip-chip QFN","s":"The die is flipped onto the lead frame on bumps instead of wire bonds; lower parasitic R and L. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ft","t":"FT","s":"Final test after packaging. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-gdbn","t":"GDBN Good die in bad neighbourhood","s":"Screening out good dies surrounded by failures; common for automotive zero-defect. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-guard-band","t":"Guard band","s":"The margin by which test limits sit inside spec limits. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-idm","t":"IDM Integrated device manufacturer","s":"A company that designs and also owns fabs and assembly/test; contrast with fabless. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ivr","t":"IVR Integrated voltage regulator","s":"A regulator inside the processor package, right at the load, cutting power-delivery impedance. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-msl","t":"MSL","s":"Moisture sensitivity level. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ocap","t":"OCAP","s":"Out-of-control action plan. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-pat","t":"PAT","s":"Part average testing: removing outliers based on the lot distribution. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-pcb","t":"PCB Printed circuit board","s":"The board carrying and connecting parts; copper area and layer count set heat spreading. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-pcn-eol-ltb","t":"PCN / EOL / LTB","s":"Product change notice / end of life / last-time buy. Also: EOL, LTB, PCN Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-pvt","t":"PVT Process, voltage, temperature corners","s":"The variation combinations design must cover: process corners (TT / FF / SS / FS / SF) × voltage × temperature. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-qfn","t":"QFN Quad / dual flat no-lead packages","s":"Small packages with an exposed thermal pad underneath and leads on the bottom edge; good thermal path. Also: DFN Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-sip","t":"SiP System in package","s":"IC, inductor and capacitors in one package; common for power modules. Also: PwrSiP Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-smt","t":"SMT Surface-mount technology","s":"Placing components on the PCB surface and reflow-soldering them. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-soic","t":"SOIC Small-outline IC packages","s":"Packages with leads on two sides; EP (exposed pad) versions cut thermal resistance a lot. Also: MSOP, SOIC-8 Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-sot-23","t":"SOT-23 Small-outline transistor packages","s":"Small leaded packages: cheap and easy to solder, but high thermal resistance (~200 °C/W or more). Also: SOT-23-5, SC-70 Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-spc","t":"SPC Statistical process control","s":"Monitoring process mean and spread with control charts and reacting when rules are violated. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-toll","t":"TOLL Power packages","s":"TOLL (TO-leadless), TO-247-4 and similar power packages; the fourth pin is usually a Kelvin source. Also: TO-247-4 Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-usl","t":"USL Upper / lower specification limit","s":"The spec bounds used to compute Cpk. Also: LSL Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-wat-pcm","t":"WAT / PCM","s":"Wafer acceptance test / process control monitor. Also: PCM, WAT Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-wlcsp","t":"WLCSP Wafer-level chip-scale package","s":"Bumps are formed on the wafer and the die is the package after sawing; package size equals die size. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"cat-test","t":"Test, reliability and EMC","s":"","g":"glossary","lv":1},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ams","t":"AMS Analogue / mixed-signal simulation","s":"Simulating digital logic and analogue circuits together, e.g. PMIC sequencing and fault handling. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-asil","t":"ASIL Automotive safety integrity level and metrics","s":"ISO 26262 levels ASIL A–D; SPFM, LFM and PMHF measure hardware-fault coverage and probability. Also: SPFM, LFM, PMHF Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ate","t":"ATE","s":"Automatic test equipment. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-bci","t":"BCI Bulk current injection","s":"An automotive immunity test that injects RF current into the harness. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-c-sam","t":"C-SAM Scanning acoustic microscopy","s":"Ultrasound that reveals delamination and voids inside a package without opening it. Also: SAM Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-cispr","t":"CISPR International Special Committee on Radio Interference","s":"The body behind EMI standards; CISPR 25 for automotive, CISPR 32 for multimedia equipment. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-dps-pmu","t":"DPS / PMU","s":"Device power supply / precision measurement unit on a tester. Also: DPS, PMU Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-elfr","t":"ELFR Early-life failure rate","s":"Short high-temperature bias on a large sample to catch infant mortality from defects. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-emi","t":"EMI Electromagnetic interference / compatibility","s":"EMI is interference a circuit emits; EMC is neither disturbing nor being disturbed. Switching supplies are major EMI sources. Also: EMC Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-esd","t":"ESD Electrostatic discharge","s":"The sudden current when a charged object touches a chip; tested as HBM, CDM (component) and IEC 61000-4-2 (system). Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-fit","t":"FIT Failures in time","s":"Failures per 10⁹ device-hours. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-golden-unit","t":"Golden unit","s":"A reference sample used to verify a measurement system. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-gr-r","t":"GR&R","s":"Gage repeatability and reproducibility of a measurement system. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-hast","t":"HAST","s":"Highly accelerated stress test (temperature and humidity). Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-hbm-cdm","t":"HBM / CDM","s":"Human body model / charged device model, two ESD models. Also: CDM, HBM Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-htgb","t":"HTGB High-temperature gate bias","s":"Long gate bias at high temperature, checking gate oxide and V_T drift. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-htol","t":"HTOL","s":"High-temperature operating life test. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-htsl","t":"HTSL High-temperature storage life","s":"Unpowered at 150–175 °C for 1000 hours, checking bond wires and intermetallics. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-kelvin","t":"Kelvin","s":"Four-wire measurement with separate force and sense. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-lisn","t":"LISN Line impedance stabilisation network","s":"The standard impedance network inserted in the supply line for conducted-EMI measurement. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-load-board","t":"Load board","s":"The interface board between tester and device. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-pc","t":"PC Preconditioning","s":"Moisture soak plus three reflows before reliability tests, simulating customer assembly. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ptc","t":"PTC Power temperature cycling","s":"Switching power on and off so the die self-heats and cools, checking metal and wire fatigue. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-rh","t":"RH Relative humidity","s":"Moisture in the air as a percentage of saturation. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-sem","t":"SEM Scanning electron microscope / energy-dispersive X-ray","s":"SEM images fine structures; EDX identifies elements. Both are FA staples. Also: EDX Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-skew-lot","t":"Skew lot","s":"A qualification lot with process parameters deliberately at the corners. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-tc","t":"TC Temperature cycling","s":"Repeated cycling between −55/−65 °C and 150 °C, checking bonds, joints and delamination. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-thb","t":"THB Temperature-humidity-bias test","s":"85 °C / 85% RH with bias for 1000 hours, checking moisture corrosion. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-uis","t":"UIS Unclamped inductive switching","s":"Switching off an inductive current to force avalanche and measure avalanche energy. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"cat-qa","t":"Quality, complaints and roles","s":"","g":"glossary","lv":1},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-8d","t":"8D","s":"Eight Disciplines, an eight-step problem-solving and reporting format. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-aba","t":"ABA ABA swap","s":"Swapping good and bad parts between boards to see whether the problem follows the part or the board. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ae","t":"AE Applications / field applications engineer","s":"AEs write app notes and reference designs; FAEs support design-ins and field issues at customers. Also: FAE Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-containment","t":"Containment","s":"Short-term measures that stop suspect parts reaching customers. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-emmi","t":"EMMI","s":"Emission microscopy, detecting faint light from defects. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-escape","t":"Escape","s":"A defective part that passes test and reaches the customer. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-fa","t":"FA","s":"Failure analysis. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-fib","t":"FIB","s":"Focused ion beam, for cross-sections and circuit edits. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-fmea","t":"FMEA","s":"Failure mode and effects analysis. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-fta","t":"FTA Fault-tree analysis","s":"Expanding every possible cause below a top failure and eliminating them with data. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-iatf","t":"IATF IATF 16949","s":"The automotive quality-management-system standard. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ntf-nff","t":"NTF / NFF","s":"No trouble found / no fault found. Also: NFF, NTF Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-obirch","t":"OBIRCH","s":"Optical beam induced resistance change, a laser-heating localisation method. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-pe","t":"PE Product engineering","s":"The role owning volume yield, trim, Cpk, cost and changes. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ppap","t":"PPAP Production part approval process","s":"The documentation and samples an automotive supplier submits before production. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-qra","t":"QRA Quality and reliability assurance","s":"The function owning qualification, change notices, outgoing quality and complaint 8Ds. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-rma","t":"RMA","s":"Return material authorisation. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-te","t":"TE Test engineering","s":"The role developing and maintaining ATE programs and hardware. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"cat-sys","t":"Systems and applications","s":"","g":"glossary","lv":1},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-adas","t":"ADAS Advanced driver-assistance systems","s":"Lane keeping, automatic braking and similar; their power must meet functional safety. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ai","t":"AI Artificial intelligence","s":"AI accelerators draw huge power, driving changes in rack power and architecture. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-am","t":"AM AM broadcast band","s":"About 530–1700 kHz; automotive DC-DCs avoid it so the radio is not disturbed. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-cold-crank","t":"Cold crank","s":"The large battery-voltage dip when an engine starts. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-cpu","t":"CPU CPU / GPU / MCU","s":"The main compute chips; GPUs drive AI compute, MCUs are small embedded controllers. Also: GPU, MCU Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-dc-bias","t":"DC bias","s":"The drop in MLCC capacitance with applied DC voltage. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ddr","t":"DDR Double data rate memory","s":"The mainstream DRAM interface; DDR5 modules carry their own PMIC. Also: DDR5 Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ecu","t":"ECU Electronic control unit","s":"A vehicle control module, e.g. engine or body controller. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-eos","t":"EOS","s":"Electrical overstress: damage from exceeding absolute maximum ratings. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-fpga","t":"FPGA Field-programmable gate array","s":"A reconfigurable digital chip with several rails and power-up rules. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-hot-loop","t":"Hot loop","s":"The high-frequency loop in a buck through which pulsed current flows. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-iot","t":"IoT Internet of Things","s":"Many small connected devices, often on batteries for years; need very low quiescent current. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-led","t":"LED LED / OLED / LCD","s":"Lighting and display devices; backlights and OLEDs need boost or negative supplies. Also: OLED, LCD Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-load-dump","t":"Load dump","s":"The high-voltage transient from an automotive alternator when its load is suddenly disconnected. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-mv","t":"MV Medium voltage","s":"Grid voltage of a few to tens of kV; the data center's incoming supply. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-poe","t":"PoE Power over Ethernet","s":"Carrying data and power (about 48 V) over an Ethernet cable. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-power-tree","t":"Power tree","s":"The conversion relationships between all rails in a system. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-pre-bias","t":"Pre-bias","s":"Starting up with voltage already on the output. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-psu","t":"PSU Power supply unit","s":"Converts mains into the DC a device needs; server PSUs are usually PFC + LLC. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-rf","t":"RF Radio frequency","s":"High-frequency wireless circuits; sensitive to supply noise and switching harmonics. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-rs-232","t":"RS-232 RS-232 serial interface","s":"An early serial standard needing positive and negative voltages, often made by charge pumps. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-soc","t":"SoC System on chip","s":"CPU, GPU, memory controller and more on one die; needs many rails with strict sequencing. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-spread-spectrum","t":"Spread spectrum","s":"Dithering the switching frequency to lower EMI peaks. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ssd","t":"SSD Solid-state drive","s":"Flash-based storage; needs several rails and power-loss protection. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-tvs","t":"TVS","s":"Transient voltage suppressor diode. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ups","t":"UPS Uninterruptible power supply","s":"Keeps power on from batteries during outages; one stage of the data-center power chain. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-usb","t":"USB Universal Serial Bus / USB Power Delivery","s":"The USB interface and its power; USB PD negotiates supply voltages from 5 to 48 V. Also: PD Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-jt","t":"ΨJT","s":"Junction-to-top thermal characterisation parameter, used to infer junction temperature from case-top temperature. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"cat-std","t":"Standards and bodies","s":"","g":"glossary","lv":1},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-80-plus","t":"80 PLUS 80 PLUS efficiency certification","s":"A PSU certification graded by efficiency at load points; Titanium is among the highest. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-aec","t":"AEC Automotive Electronics Council standards","s":"AEC-Q100 qualifies automotive ICs, AEC-Q101 discretes, AEC-Q001 is PAT; suffixes are individual test methods. Also: AEC-Q100, AEC-Q101, AEC-Q001, AEC-Q100-002, AEC-Q100-004, AEC-Q100-011, AEC-Q100-012 Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-aqg","t":"AQG AQG 324","s":"The qualification guideline for automotive power modules. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-iec","t":"IEC International Electrotechnical Commission","s":"IEC 61000-4-2 is the system-level ESD test standard. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-iso","t":"ISO International Organization for Standardization","s":"ISO 26262 functional safety; ISO 16750 and ISO 7637 automotive electrical environment and transients. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-j-std-020","t":"J-STD-020 Moisture-sensitivity classification","s":"The IPC/JEDEC standard defining MSL levels and reflow conditions. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-jedec","t":"JEDEC JEDEC Solid State Technology Association","s":"The body setting memory and semiconductor package, test and reliability standards, e.g. JESD47, JESD51. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-jesd46","t":"JESD46 JEDEC standard numbers","s":"JESD46 change notices, JESD47 qualification, JESD48 discontinuation, JESD51 thermal measurement, JESD78 latch-up. Also: JESD47, JESD48, JESD51, JESD78 Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-mipi","t":"MIPI MIPI Alliance","s":"The body defining mobile interfaces such as SPMI. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"cat-wbg","t":"Wide bandgap and data centers","s":"","g":"glossary","lv":1},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-bpd","t":"BPD","s":"Basal plane dislocation, the source of SiC bipolar degradation. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-cmti","t":"CMTI","s":"Common-mode transient immunity of an isolated driver. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-coolsic","t":"CoolSiC Infineon SiC / GaN product lines","s":"CoolSiC is Infineon's SiC MOSFETs and diodes; CoolGaN its GaN transistors. Also: CoolGaN Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-dpt","t":"DPT","s":"Double-pulse test, the standard method for switching characterisation. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-gan","t":"GaN Gallium nitride","s":"A wide-bandgap semiconductor with the fastest switching; suits high-frequency, soft-switched DC/DC. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-hold-up-time","t":"Hold-up time","s":"How long the output stays in spec after input power is lost. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-hvdc","t":"HVDC","s":"High-voltage direct-current distribution. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-ibc","t":"IBC","s":"Intermediate bus converter (e.g. 48 V → 12 V). Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-llc","t":"LLC","s":"Inductor-inductor-capacitor resonant converter, common for soft-switched isolated DC/DC. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-seb","t":"SEB","s":"Single-event burnout caused by a particle strike. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-sic","t":"SiC Silicon carbide","s":"A wide-bandgap semiconductor with about 10× silicon's critical field; suits high voltage, high power, hard switching. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-sst","t":"SST","s":"Solid-state transformer. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-totem-pole-pfc","t":"Totem-pole PFC","s":"Bridgeless totem-pole power-factor correction, a high-efficiency PFC topology without a rectifier bridge. Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-wide-bandgap-wbg","t":"Wide bandgap (WBG)","s":"Semiconductors with a bandgap clearly larger than silicon's, such as SiC and GaN. Also: WBG, Wide bandgap Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"glossary.html","a":"g-xt","t":".XT .XT interconnect","s":"Infineon's diffusion-solder die-attach technology for lower thermal resistance and longer power-cycling life. Also: XT Details →","g":"glossary","lv":3},{"p":"lab.html","a":"bode","t":"LDO stability: Bode plot and ESR window","s":"phase margin crossover output pole ESR zero tunnel of death ceramic tantalum internal compensation minimum capacitance oscillation","g":"lab","lv":1},{"p":"lab.html","a":"psrr","t":"PSRR frequency-response simulation","s":"power supply rejection headroom dropout buck ripple switching frequency post-regulation PMOS NMOS","g":"lab","lv":1},{"p":"lab.html","a":"transient","t":"Load-transient simulation","s":"undershoot overshoot ESR loop bandwidth phase margin ringing slew rate load regulation","g":"lab","lv":1},{"p":"lab.html","a":"thermal","t":"Dropout, dissipation and junction temperature","s":"dropout thermal θJA package SOT-23 DFN QFN thermal shutdown TSD hiccup efficiency","g":"lab","lv":1},{"p":"lab.html","a":"buck","t":"Buck inductor ripple, output ripple and efficiency","s":"duty cycle ripple current saturation current Isat CCM DCM PFM FPWM t_ON,min efficiency curve","g":"lab","lv":1},{"p":"lab.html","a":"mlcc","t":"MLCC DC bias and effective capacitance","s":"ceramic capacitor DC bias X5R X7R Y5V case size 0402 0603 rated voltage temperature ageing tolerance","g":"lab","lv":1},{"p":"lab.html","a":"trim","t":"Trim, yield and Cpk simulation","s":"trim code LSB bits package shift pre-compensation CP FT yield Cpk Monte Carlo","g":"lab","lv":1},{"p":"quiz.html","a":"","t":"Power IC quiz","s":"quiz questions self-check architecture process design production test applications complaints","g":"quiz","lv":1}];
